The mechanisms discussed here are \emph{correlational diagnostics} that illuminate but do not fully explain the $15$--$17{\times}$ severity divergence between strategies. We present importance score flattening as one such diagnostic rather than a complete causal explanation. The consistent Gini decrease across all conditions provides a necessary background---aggregate attention concentration drops uniformly under head pruning regardless of eviction strategy (importance $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$: $-0.030$ to $-0.081$; random $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$: $-0.031$ to $-0.071$; ${<}10\%$ difference). However, severity diverges by $15$--$17{\times}$ between strategies despite near-identical Gini changes, indicating that concentration collapse alone is insufficient. The critical differentiator is \emph{structural specificity}: importance-based pruning systematically removes high-attention-score heads, disrupting the attention pattern structure that KV eviction relies on, while random pruning preserves more structural diversity. The quantitative gap between score-level changes ($6$--$16\%$ relative Gini reduction from a DeepSeek baseline of $\widetilde{\mathrm{Gini}} \approx 0.50$; Qwen baseline $\approx 0.57$) and outcome-level amplification ($G{=}7.5$--$79.8{\times}$) indicates additional nonlinear factors (e.g., cascading effects across layers) that warrant dedicated investigation.  % exp_id: c4_entropy; DS baseline Gini 0.496, QW 0.571; imp ΔGini -0.029 to -0.082, rand -0.031 to -0.071

\subsection{Importance Score Flattening}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse-main}

Head pruning systematically reduces the discriminative power of cumulative importance scores, suggesting a mechanism for the phase-strategy interaction. The Gini coefficient decreases by $0.030$--$0.081$ (DeepSeek, $6$--$17\%$ relative to dense baseline) and $0.010$--$0.020$ (Qwen, $2$--$4\%$) after pruning, with consistent negative deltas in all 16 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}). Critically, random eviction shows near-identical Gini drops at matched sparsity (DeepSeek $s{=}0.25$: importance $-0.065$ vs.\ random $-0.060$; $s{=}0.30$: $-0.081$ vs.\ $-0.071$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.046$ for both strategies), confirming that importance score flattening is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not the eviction strategy. Yet importance-based severity is $15$--$17{\times}$ larger than random at matched sparsity---the amplification arises because importance-based eviction \emph{depends on} the very scores that pruning degrades, while random eviction's decisions are score-independent. % exp_id: c4_entropy; DS imp vs rand ΔGini: s025 -0.065 vs -0.060, s030 -0.081 vs -0.071

This pattern---\emph{importance score flattening}---is consistent with importance-based eviction degenerating into noise-sensitive selection when score distributions flatten. The effect is phase-dependent: in low-sparsity phases, attention is already broadly distributed, so pruning-induced flattening has maximum impact; in high-sparsity phases, concentration is preserved. This observation is independently corroborated by \cite{hies-2025}, who report importance score collapse under heavy single-axis pruning; our results extend this to the compound setting, where flattening is amplified by the interaction of two compression axes and modulated by reasoning phase. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

To provide quantitative evidence for structural specificity, we analyzed the attention Gini coefficients of importance-pruned versus retained heads. Importance pruning (Frobenius norm criterion) systematically removes heads with lower Gini coefficients---i.e., heads with more dispersed attention patterns (Mann-Whitney $U$, $p < 10^{-28}$; rank-biserial $r = 0.62$--$0.76$ across all 8 conditions). Random pruning, by contrast, samples heads with Gini indistinguishable from the population mean (200-trial verification). We acknowledge that the Frobenius norm and Gini coefficient share sensitivity to attention weight magnitude, making their correlation partially expected; the key evidence is the consistent large effect size ($r > 0.6$) indicating strong structural separation between pruned and retained head sets. % exp_id: w4_structural_specificity

We note that this evidence is correlational; causal validation through controlled manipulation of importance-score entropy is an important direction for future work. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

\subsection{Entropy Redistribution}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution-main}

As a supplementary observation, head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads on DeepSeek ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.047, -0.040]$; Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}). However, entropy redistribution shows model-dependent behavior---the direction reverses on Qwen at $s{=}0.25$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = +0.045$)---replicating in only 2/4 Qwen conditions and thus only partially consistent. Discrimination collapse remains the more robust, model-independent correlate, though it partially accounts for rather than fully explains the severity divergence. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We showed that compound sparsity severity---the super-additive interaction between head pruning and KV cache eviction---is not phase-invariant but exhibits an emergent phase-strategy interaction spanning over an order of magnitude within a single generation ($n{=}496$/$475$ problems). Under importance-based eviction, the amplification ratio $G$ ranges from $7.5{\times}$ to $79.8{\times}$ across sparsity levels, with paired median differences $\Delta$ from $0.39$ to $8.48$ (DeepSeek) and $0.07$ to $3.16$ (Qwen), all $p < 10^{-13}$. Random eviction develops a detectable phase gradient only above $s \approx 0.20$, but $3$--$17{\times}$ weaker than importance-based where both strategies are significant ($s \geq 0.25$). A crossover on DeepSeek at $s{=}0.25$---where importance-based eviction reverses ranking across phases ($2.6{\times}$ reversal)---demonstrates that no single strategy dominates uniformly; Qwen does not exhibit this crossover, indicating model dependence. The pattern replicates on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct at all four sparsity levels; suggestive preliminary evidence from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM) indicates the phenomenon may extend beyond Qwen2, though at ${\sim}1/40$ the magnitude and with a weaker strategy contrast. Discrimination collapse provides a correlational diagnostic supported by both Qwen2-based models, with structural specificity of importance-based pruning as the key differentiator from random eviction---despite near-identical Gini drops ($<10\%$ difference), severity diverges by $15$--$17{\times}$.
These findings establish the phase-dependent nature of compound severity as a prerequisite and diagnostic foundation for future phase-aware mitigation strategies. Extending this analysis to code reasoning (HumanEval, LiveCodeBench) and science reasoning (GPQA), validating the discrimination collapse mechanism through causal intervention, and characterizing asymmetric compound interactions are promising directions for future work.

