\paragraph{Model scale and architecture.}
All experiments are conducted at the 7--8B parameter scale. Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested. Preliminary cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) partially confirms the qualitative pattern beyond the Qwen2 architecture---monotonically increasing $d_z$ with within-importance phase gradient $\sim$1.0---but with substantially weaker interaction magnitude ($\sim$1/40 of DeepSeek) and mixed statistical evidence (only $s{=}0.30$ significant after Bonferroni correction). The pattern has been tested on two architecture families (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM); comprehensive validation across more diverse architectures (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention) and larger scales is needed for full generalization claims.

\paragraph{Task coverage.}
We evaluate exclusively on MATH-500, a mathematical reasoning benchmark. MATH-500 is a suitable first testbed because it elicits structured reasoning with clear phase transitions (exploratory vs.\ structured computation), supports per-problem evaluation enabling paired statistical tests, and spans sufficient difficulty to produce meaningful variation in sequence length and attention dynamics. However, different reasoning tasks---code generation (e.g., HumanEval, LiveCodeBench), science reasoning (e.g., GPQA), multi-hop question answering---may induce different phase structures with different sparsity distributions, potentially altering the severity gradient profile. Whether the phase-strategy interaction documented here extends to these domains is an open question.

\paragraph{Symmetric sparsity assumption.}
Our analysis applies symmetric sparsity rates: the same fraction $s$ is used for both head pruning and KV cache eviction. Practical deployments may use asymmetric budgets (e.g., aggressive head pruning with conservative cache eviction, or vice versa). The interaction dynamics under asymmetric compound sparsity may differ from the symmetric case studied here; investigating asymmetric compound interactions is a natural extension.

\paragraph{Mechanism.}
The discrimination collapse mechanism is supported by correlational evidence (consistent Gini/CV reduction across all conditions); causal validation through controlled entropy manipulation is needed. The entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 Qwen conditions, suggesting model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Perplexity vs.\ accuracy.}
Our severity metric is perplexity-based (II), which captures reasoning process quality through next-token prediction degradation. We chose perplexity because its per-token granularity enables the phase-level decomposition central to our analysis; accuracy is binary per-problem and cannot be meaningfully decomposed by reasoning phase. Whether the PPL-level severity gradient translates to downstream accuracy requires future validation through generation under compound sparsity conditions. Even if final answers survive due to natural phase shielding (\S\ref{sec:answer-region}), degraded intermediate reasoning may produce silent reasoning errors, affect reliability on harder problems, reduce interpretability, and impact open-ended reasoning tasks where no structured answer format provides natural shielding.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our experiments span $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics. We do not report inference throughput or latency measurements, which are necessary for a complete deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization. Extended limitations in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Discrimination Collapse Details}
\label{app:discrimination-collapse-details}

This cross-model consistency (Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}) establishes discrimination collapse as a robust consequence of compound sparsity. When importance scores lose discriminative power, thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction. The same pattern holds under random eviction (DeepSeek: $-0.031$ to $-0.071$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.046$), confirming that discrimination collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not of the eviction strategy. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen


\section{Extended Discussion}
\label{app:extended-discussion}

\paragraph{On the predictability of phase-modulated severity.}
The compound interaction is not uniformly fragile---it is selectively fragile in a phase-dependent manner ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\text{high}} < 0.06$ at $s \leq 0.30$). % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; high-phase medians: 0.006, 0.015, 0.032, 0.053

\paragraph{Random masking as falsification control.}
At $n{=}500$, random eviction develops a detectable but weak phase gradient above $s \approx 0.20$: non-significant at $s \leq 0.20$ for both models, but significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (DS: $\Delta{=}0.26$--$0.51$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.006$; QW: $\Delta{=}0.39$--$0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$). Where significant, the random phase gradient is $3$--$39{\times}$ weaker than importance-based, confirming that the dominant amplification is strategy-specific. % exp_id: n500_unified_final; DS rand s025 0.257 p=0.006, s030 0.510 p=0.006; QW rand s025 0.393, s030 0.913

\paragraph{Entropy mechanism boundary conditions.}
Directly measuring per-head entropy shifts across architecturally distinct models---and identifying what training-time properties determine whether entropy concentrates or disperses under head pruning---would clarify the boundary conditions of the full mechanism.


\section{Pilot Mitigation Experiment}
\label{app:pilot-mitigation}

We explored oracle phase-aware budget reallocation, shifting sparsity budget from high-severity low-sparsity phases to low-severity high-sparsity phases. At $s{=}0.25$, oracle recovery---the fraction of compound interaction eliminated by phase-aware allocation---reached 18\% (Wilcoxon $p = 0.002$), below the pre-registered 30\% gate for claiming practical mitigation. A heuristic variant achieved 32\% recovery ($p = 0.024$), but 95\% CIs for both conditions span zero. These results indicate that phase-aware budget reallocation alone is insufficient to neutralize the severity gradient; effective mitigation likely requires joint optimization of budget allocation and eviction strategy selection across phases.


\section{Extended Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

\paragraph{Model scale.}
All experiments are conducted at the 7B parameter scale.
Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested.
Larger models may exhibit different attention redundancy structures that alter the discrimination collapse dynamics (and the model-dependent entropy component may behave differently at scale).


\paragraph{Architectural scope.}
Our two models---DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct---share the Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
The cross-training-paradigm replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}) establishes that the findings are not artifacts of a single model's weights, though both models share the same Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
Preliminary cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) shows qualitatively consistent within-importance phase gradients ($d_z \sim 1.0$) but a substantially weaker strategy $\times$ phase interaction than in Qwen2-based architectures; random pruning also exhibits a non-trivial phase gradient on LLaMA, reducing the strategy contrast. This partial confirmation ($s{=}0.30$ Wilcoxon $p_{\text{Bonf}}{=}0.029$; $s \leq 0.25$ non-significant) suggests the qualitative pattern may generalize but quantitative impact is architecture-dependent. Comprehensive cross-architecture validation across more diverse designs (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention architectures) and scales remains an important next step.


\paragraph{Task coverage.}
We evaluate exclusively on MATH-500, a mathematical reasoning benchmark.
Different reasoning tasks---code generation, commonsense reasoning, multi-hop question answering---may induce different phase structures with different sparsity distributions, potentially altering the severity gradient profile.
Generalizing the phase-strategy interaction to broader reasoning domains is an important next step.


\paragraph{Statistical scope.}
Bootstrap confidence intervals use $B{=}10{,}000$ resamples (seed$=$42). The primary analysis uses $n{=}496$ paired problems for DeepSeek (4 excluded) and $n{=}475$ for Qwen (25 excluded); the permutation test (Table~\ref{tab:permutation}) uses $n{=}49$ from an initial profiling subset. % exp_id: n500_unified_final; deepseek_bf16_permtest
The severity difference distribution is right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to $43{:}1$), motivating our use of medians and nonparametric tests rather than parametric alternatives. % exp_id: n500_unified_final
All four profiling-subset permutation tests reject $H_0$ after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$; Table~\ref{tab:permutation}), with CI lower bounds increasing from $0.19$ to $6.70$---confirming the effect direction at every sparsity level. At $n{=}496$, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests confirm all conditions with $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ (Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d}). The permutation CIs at higher sparsity are wider (e.g., $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ at $s{=}0.30$), reflecting genuine variance in per-problem severity amplification. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; permutation CIs: s015=[0.19,0.62], s020=[0.69,1.49], s025=[1.66,6.64], s030=[6.70,16.29]

At $n{=}500$, random eviction on both models becomes significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (Qwen: $\Delta{=}0.39$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$ at $s{=}0.25$; $\Delta{=}0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ at $s{=}0.30$; bootstrap CIs exclude zero). % exp_id: n500_unified_final; QW rand s025 [0.236, 0.553], s030 [0.613, 1.258]
These effects are genuine but $2.7$--$20{\times}$ weaker than importance-based eviction at the same sparsity levels, consistent with a weak phase gradient that is detectable at sufficient sample size but not the primary driver of compound severity.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our experiments span $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, a moderate range representative of practical deployment targets.
More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics---the sub-exponential growth pattern suggests the severity gradient may plateau, but this remains unverified.
Conversely, the lower boundary at which phase-modulated amplification first emerges is not yet characterized.


\paragraph{No throughput evaluation.}
We do not report inference throughput or latency, which are necessary for deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization.

